18e1d4dc1f"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Euclid preparation - XXXVIII. Spectroscopy of active galactic nuclei with NISP","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-083.20-742183","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We present a choreographic framework for modelling and analysing concurrent probabilistic systems based on the PRISM model-checker. This is achieved through the development of a choreography language, which is a specification language that allows to describe the desired interactions within a concurrent system from a global viewpoint. Employing choreographies provides a clear and comprehensive view of system interactions, enabling the discernment of process flow and detection of potential errors, thus ensuring accurate execution and enhancing system reliability. We equip our language with a probabilistic semantics and then define a formal encoding into the PRISM language and discuss its correctness. Properties of programs written in our choreographic language can be model-checked by the PRISM model-checker via their translation into the PRISM language. Finally, we implement a compiler for our language and demonstrate its practical applicability via examples drawn from the use cases featured in the PRISM website.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Veschetti, Adele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759403"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Darmstadt University of Technology"],"keyword":["Choreographic Programming","Model checking"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a probabilistic choreography language for prism","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85197248837","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759403","identifier":[{"id":"85197248837","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5eee6e95-83f5-44f7-80df-8f3fe4075c1e"],"keyword":["Choreographic programming","Formal encoding and compilation","Model checking","PRISM model-checker","Probabilistic concurrent systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-353374BC68A26337D19EAB5109AB41E0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"darmstadt university of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Darmstadt University of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Adele","key":"veschettipau","last":"Veschetti","name":"Veschetti, Adele","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-62697-5_2","eisbn":["9783031626975"],"isbn":["9783031626968"],"pages":"20-37","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"LNCS","volume":"14676"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85197248837"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5eee6e95-83f5-44f7-80df-8f3fe4075c1e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Probabilistic Choreography Language for PRISM","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759403","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Session types specify the protocols that communicating processes must follow in a concurrent system. When composing two or more processes, a session typing system must check whether such processes are compatible, i.e., that all sent messages are eventually received and no deadlock ever occurs. After the propositions-as-types paradigm, relating session types to linear logic, previous work has shown that duality, in the binary case, and more generally coherence, in the multiparty case, are sufficient syntactic conditions to guarantee compatibility for two or more processes, yet do not characterise all compatible set of processes. In this work, we generalise duality/coherence to a notion of forwarder compatibility. Forwarders are specified as a restricted family of proofs in linear logic, therefore defining a specific set of processes that can act as middleware by transfering messages without using them. As such, they can guide a network of processes to execute asynchronously. Our main result establishes forwarder compatibility as a sufficient and necessary condition to fully capture all well-typed multiparty compatible processes.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Marin, Sonia","Schürmann, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712558"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Birmingham"],"keyword":["Linear Logic","Process Compatibility","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a logical interpretation of asynchronous multiparty compatibility","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.16240","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712558","identifier":[{"id":"85175796960","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-db25bd1d-2b75-4c3e-a092-486e2ae109c7"],"keyword":["Linear logic","Process Compatibility","Session Types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-90CA0AB446D772ED23B0B098EC9AB491"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.16240","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of birmingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Birmingham","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sonia","key":"marinpau","last":"Marin","name":"Marin, Sonia","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-45784-5_7","eisbn":["9783031457845"],"isbn":["9783031457838"],"issn":["03029743"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"A Logical Interpretation of Asynchronous Multiparty Compatibility","volume":"14330"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85175796960"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-db25bd1d-2b75-4c3e-a092-486e2ae109c7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Logical Interpretation of Asynchronous Multiparty Compatibility","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712558","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Apoptosis is a form of regulated cell death (RCD) that involves proteases of the caspase family. Pharmacological and genetic strategies that experimentally inhibit or delay apoptosis in mammalian systems have elucidated the key contribution of this process not only to (post-)embryonic development and adult tissue homeostasis, but also to the etiology of multiple human disorders. Consistent with this notion, while defects in the molecular machinery for apoptotic cell death impair organismal development and promote oncogenesis, the unwarranted activation of apoptosis promotes cell loss and tissue damage in the context of various neurological, cardiovascular, renal, hepatic, infectious, neoplastic and inflammatory conditions. Here, the Nomenclature Committee on Cell Death (NCCD) gathered to critically summarize an abundant pre-clinical literature mechanistically linking the core apoptotic apparatus to organismal homeostasis in the context of disease.","author_count":228,"contributors":["Vitale, Ilio","Pietrocola, Federico","Guilbaud, Emma","Aaronson, Stuart A.","Abrams, John M.","Adam, Dieter","Agostini, Massimiliano","Agostinis, Patrizia","Alnemri, Emad S.","Altucci, Lucia","Amelio, Ivano","Andrews, David W.","Aqeilan, Rami I.","Arama, Eli","Baehrecke, Eric H.","Balachandran, Siddharth","Bano, Daniele","Barlev, Nickolai A.","Bartek, Jiri","Bazan, Nicolas G.","Becker, Christoph","Bernassola, Francesca","Bertrand, Mathieu J.M.","Bianchi, Marco E.","Blagosklonny, Mikhail V.","Blander, J. Magarian","Blandino, Giovanni","Blomgren, Klas","Borner, Christoph","Bortner, Carl D.","Bove, Pierluigi","Boya, Patricia","Brenner, Catherine","Broz, Petr","Brunner, Thomas","Damgaard, Rune Busk","Calin, George A.","Campanella, Michelangelo","Candi, Eleonora","Carbone, Michele","Carmona-Gutierrez, Didac","Cecconi, Francesco","Chan, Francis K.M.","Chen, Guo Qiang","Chen, Quan","Chen, Youhai H.","Cheng, Emily H.","Chipuk, Jerry E.","Cidlowski, John A.","Ciechanover, Aaron","Ci