{"abstract":"In Choreographic Programming, a distributed system is programmed by giving a choreography, a global description of its interactions, instead of separately specifying the behaviour of each of its processes. Process implementations in terms of a distributed language can then be automatically projected from a choreography. We present Linear Compositional Choreographies (LCC), a proof theory for reasoning about programs that modularly combine choreographies with processes. Using LCC, we logically reconstruct a semantics and a projection procedure for programs. For the first time, we also obtain a procedure for extracting choreographies from process terms.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Montesi, Fabrizio","Schürmann, Carsten"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-038412"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Montesi, Fabrizio"],"keyword":["Choreographies","Curry Howard Isomorphism","Linear Logic","Programming Languages"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"choreographies logically","src":["itu","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83512273/main_Distributed_Computing.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-038412","identifier":[{"id":"85014780411","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f7135657-c5c5-4002-9c1e-09b2a3cfb963","sdu-ccd494a0-74af-4b09-989b-9de5e268c89e"],"keyword":["Choreographies","Curry–Howard isomorphism","Linear logic","Programming languages"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-F971E16A74EE77E20EFCEC3CBB3366EE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83512273/main_Distributed_Computing.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/153104720/Choreographies_logically.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","locid":"81d9220c-ba43-48b1-aa21-129c942a9c92","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","orcid":"0000-0003-4666-901X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00446-017-0295-1","issn":["01782770","14320452"],"issn_org":["01782770"],"issue":"1","journal":"Distributed Computing","pages":"51-67","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Distributed Computing","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85014780411"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f7135657-c5c5-4002-9c1e-09b2a3cfb963","sdu-ccd494a0-74af-4b09-989b-9de5e268c89e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Choreographies, logically","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-038412","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Session types are behavioural types for guaranteeing that concurrent programs are free from basic communication errors. Recent work has shown that asynchronous session subtyping is undecidable. However, since session types have become popular in mainstream programming languages in which asynchronous communication is the norm rather than the exception, it is crucial to detect significant decidable subtyping relations. Previous work considered extremely restrictive fragments in which limitations were imposed to the size of communication buffer (at most 1) or to the possibility to express multiple choices (disallowing them completely in one of the compared types). In this work, for the first time, we show decidability of a fragment that does not impose any limitation on communication buffers and allows both the compared types to include multiple choices for either input or output, thus yielding a fragment which is more significant from an applicability viewpoint. In general, we study the boundary between decidability and undecidability by considering several fragments of subtyping. Notably, we show that subtyping remains undecidable even if restricted to not using output covariance and input contravariance.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bravetti, Mario","Carbone, Marco","Zavattaro, Gianluigi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384090"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bologna"],"keyword":["Asynchronous Subtyping","Session types","Undecidability"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the boundary between decidability and undecidability of asynchronous session subtyping","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83512280/main_Theoretical_Computer_Science.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384090","identifier":[{"id":"85042153389","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c14af606-875b-4a55-a816-fa99fae8c49d"],"keyword":["Asynchronous Subtyping","Session Types","Undecidability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E363042A847660A3E2659FC98C428DFB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83512280/main_Theoretical_Computer_Science.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"university of bologna","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bologna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mario","key":"bravettipau","last":"Bravetti","name":"Bravetti, Mario","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gianluigi","key":"zavattaropau","last":"Zavattaro","name":"Zavattaro, Gianluigi","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.tcs.2018.02.010","issn":["03043975"],"journal":"Theoretical Computer Science","pages":"19-51","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Theoretical Computer Science","volume":"722"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85042153389"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c14af606-875b-4a55-a816-fa99fae8c49d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the boundary between decidability and undecidability of asynchronous session subtyping","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384090","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Nomenclatural type definitions are one of the most important concepts in biological nomenclature. Being physical objects that can be re-studied by other researchers, types permanently link taxonomy (an artificial agreement to classify biological diversity) with nomenclature (an artificial agreement to name biological diversity). Two proposals to amend the International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants (ICN), allowing DNA sequences alone (of any region and extent) to serve as types of taxon names for voucherless fungi (mainly putative taxa from environmental DNA sequences), have been submitted to be voted on at the 11th International Mycological Congress (Puerto Rico, July 2018). We consider various genetic processes affecting the distribution of alleles among taxa and find that alleles may not consistently and uniquely represent the species within which they are contained. Should the proposals be accepted, the meaning of nomenclatural types would change in a fundamental way from physical objects as sources of data to the data themselves. Such changes are conducive to irreproducible science, the potential typification on artefactual data, and massive creation of names with low information content, ultimately causing nomenclatural instability and unnecessary work for future researchers that would stall future explorations of fungal diversity. We conclude that the acceptance of DNA sequences alone as types of names of taxa, under the terms used in the current proposals, is unnecessary and would not solve the problem of naming putative taxa known only from DNA sequences in a scientifically defensible way. As an alternative, we highlight the use of formulas for naming putative taxa (candidate taxa) that do not require any modification of the ICN.","author_count":412,"contributors":["Zamora, Juan Carlos","Svensson, Mans","Kirschner, Roland","Olariaga, Ibai","Ryman, Svengunnar","Alberto Parra, Luis","Geml, Jozsef","Rosling, Anna","Adamcik, Sla