{"abstract":"Wadler introduced Classical Processes (CP), a calculus based on a propositions-as-types correspondence between propositions of classical linear logic and session types. Carbone et al. introduced Multiparty Classical Processes, a calculus that generalises CP to multiparty session types, by replacing the duality of classical linear logic (relating two types) with a more general notion of coherence (relating an arbitrary number of types). This paper introduces variants of CP and MCP, plus a new intermediate calculus of Globally-governed Classical Processes (GCP). We show a tight relation between these three calculi, giving semantics-preserving translations from GCP to CP and from MCP to GCP. The translation from GCP to CP interprets a coherence proof as an arbiter process that mediates communications in a session, while MCP adds annotations that permit processes to communicate directly without centralised control. © Marco Carbone, Sam Lindley, Fabrizio Montesi, Carsten Schürmann, and Philip Wadler; licensed under Creative Commons License CC-BY.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Lindley, Sam","Montesi, Fabrizio","Schürmann, Carsten","Wadler, Philip"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-594094"],"event":{"acronym":"CONCUR","end":"2016-08-26","place":"Québec City, ca","start":"2016-08-23","title":"27th International Conference on Concurrency Theory","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The University of Edinburgh"],"keyword":["Linear Logic","Multiparty Session Types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"coherence generalises duality a logical explanation of multiparty session types","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CONCUR.2016.33","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-594094","identifier":[{"id":"85012907940","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-48d05001-80c1-4f13-9f6f-82b33da844fe"],"keyword":["Linear logic","Multiparty Session Types, Linear Logic, Propositions as Types","Multiparty session types","Propositions as types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-3-3C311C93112302E1F106231191870530"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CONCUR.2016.33","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/129416303/Coherence_generalises_duality.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"the university of edinburgh","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"The University of Edinburgh","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Compute