{"abstract":"Over the last decade, global descriptions have been successfully employed for the verification and implementation of communicating systems, respectively as protocol specifications and choreographies. In this work, we bring these two practices together by proposing a purely-global programming model. We show a novel interpretation of asynchrony and parallelism in a global setting and develop a typing discipline that verifies choreographies against protocol specifications, based on multiparty sessions. Exploiting the nature of global descriptions, our type system defines a new class of deadlock-free concurrent systems (deadlock-freedom-by-design), provides type inference, and supports session mobility. We give a notion of Endpoint Projection (EPP) which generates correct entity code (as pi-calculus terms) from a choreography. Finally, we evaluate our approach by providing a prototype implementation for a concrete programming language and by applying it to some examples from multicore and service-oriented programming.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Montesi, Fabrizio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382289"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Choreography","Concurrency","Sessions","Types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"deadlock freedom by design multiparty asynchronous global programming","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-382289","ids":["itu-44cfe3c8-abca-4565-aba1-522e95f3485c"],"keyword":["Choreography","Concurrency","Sessions","Types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-23B3B71D4F97B7B3019CEA1C78247F89"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Fabrizio","key":"montesipau","last":"Montesi","name":"Montesi, Fabrizio","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["15232867"],"issue":"1","journal":"ACM / SIGPLAN Notices","pages":"263-274","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM / SIGPLAN Notices","title_org":"A C M / S I G P L A N Notices","volume":"48"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84877904084"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44cfe3c8-abca-4565-aba1-522e95f3485c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Deadlock-freedom-by-design: Multiparty Asynchronous Global Programming","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382289","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Despite much work on sessions and session types in non- adversarial contexts, session-like behavior given an active adversary has not received an adequate definition and proof methods. We provide a syntactic property that guarantees that a protocol has session-respecting executions. Any uncompromised subset of the participants are still guar- anteed that their interaction will respect sessions. A protocol transfor- mation turns any protocol into a session-respecting protocol. We do this via a general theory of separability. Our main theorem ap- plies to different separability requirements, and characterizes when we can separate protocol executions sufficiently to meet a particular require- ment. This theorem also gives direct proofs of some old and new protocol composition results. Thus, our theory of separability appears to cover protocol composition and session-like behavior within a uniform frame- work, and gives a general pattern for reasoning about independence.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Guttman, Joshua"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382862"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Worcester Polytechnic Institute"],"keyword":["Protocol Composition","Protocol Transformation","Separability Theory","Session Respecting Executions","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sessions and separability in security protocols","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84874410337","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382862","identifier":[{"id":"84874410337","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-6b09fa5d-cf7f-4697-94f5-2b9e3471623f"],"keyword":["Protocol Composition","Protocol Transformation","Separability Theory","Session Types","Session-Respecting Executions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5287A4D83B39A1FBBD4B5D7CBFE60656"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"worcester polytechnic institute","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Worcester Polytechnic Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Joshua","key":"guttmanpau","last":"Guttman","name":"Guttman, Joshua","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9783642368295"],"pages":"267-286","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Second International Conference, POST 2013, Held as Part of the European Joint Conferences on Theory and Practice of Software, ETAPS 2013,","volume":"7796"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84874410337"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-6b09fa5d-cf7f-4697-94f5-2b9e3471623f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Sessions and Separability in Security Protocols","type":"Conference Paper","